Titre$ Field key to commoner and/or distinctive mosses and liverworts
Rem$ Ian Atherton, Sam Bosanquet & Mark Lawley - Mosses and Liverworts of Britain and Ireland : a field guide. British Bryological Society, 2010
Com$ Adaptation par A. Paris, 2013

1,5$ Plant without distinct leaves. $$s"Hornworts & Thallose liverworts"
1,2$ Plant with distinct leaves
2,24$ Leaves arranged either strictly in 2 ranks or in 3 ranks (one on each side of the stem and one in mid-ventral line) ; the leaves most commonly round or 2-lobed, without nerves (sometimes leaves are so deeply divided as to appear 4- or 5-ranked. $$s"Leafy liverworts"
2,3$ Leaves not arranged strictly in ranks (except in Fissidens and Distichium, which have nerved leaves, and Schistostega, which has a shining green protonema) ; the leaves never 2-lobed, most often tapering to an acute or rounded tip, with or without nerves
3,0$ Plants consisting of numerous upright stems on which branches are in groups arranged spirally down the stem ; all grow in bogs or wet ground, and look pale when dry, but are vivid green, orange-brown or purple-red when moist, and hold water like a sponge $Sphagnum
3,4$ Plants never showing the regular, whorled branching of Sphagnum ; habits and habitats various
4,78$ Separate, upright or ascending shoots which are simple, forked, or with a few short lateral branches ; leaves very seldom like thin, glossy scales ; seta arising from tip of shoots (except in some species of Fissidens, Racomitrium, etc.). The acrocarp/pleurocarp split may appear difficult at first. Looking at the gallery of images on pp. 9-18 may help. $$s"Acrocarpous mosses"
4,285$ Mat-like growth of much-branched stems, which are irregularly intertwined and most often prostrate or arched ; branching most often irregular or pinnate (occasionally growth is upright from a horizontal stolon, branched above to give plant a miniature tree habit, e.g. Climacium) ; leaves most often like thin, glossy scales ; seta not arising from tip of stems. The acrocarp/pleurocarp split may appear difficult at first. Looking at the gallery of images on pp. 9-18 may help. $$s"Pleurocarpous mosses"
5,6$ Thallus with gemmae in very obvious receptacles
5,8$ Thallus without gemmae in receptacles
6,7$ Gemmae visible in open-topped receptacles ; no gemmae on thallus tips ; upper surface of thallus with raised pores
6,0$ Gemmae hidden in receptacles like flasks with a long neck ; thallus tips with star-shaped gemmae on the surface; upper surface of thallus without raised pores $Blasia pusilla
7,0$ Gemmae in cup-shaped receptacles $Marchantia polymorpha
7,0$ Gemmae in crescent-shaped receptacles $Lunularia cruciata
8,9$ Upper surface of thallus with polygonal markings ; a more or less raised pore in centre of each polygon
8,11$ Upper surface of thallus without such markings and pores
9,0$ Thalli usually > 1 cm wide ; plants strongly scented ; pores on upper surface easily visible to naked eye ; scales on lower surface not overlapping each other $Conocephalum spp.
9,10$ Thalli always < 1 cm wide ; plant scentless ; pores on upper surface hardly visible to naked eye ; scales on lower surface overlapping one another
10,0$ Diamond-shaped marking prominent ; thallus tastes peppery ; young female heads dull green and 4-lobed $Preissia quadrata
10,0$ Diamond-shaped markings very faint ; thallus not peppery to taste ; young female heads bright green and with 5-7 lobes $Reboulia hemisphaerica
11,12$ Plants growing in pale green rosettes on bare soils, or floating in pools
11,15$ Plants deep or vivid green ; habit various
12,0$ Upper surface of thallus with round-topped cells and a spongy appearance ; on wet mud $Riccia cavernosa
12,13$ Upper surface of thallus with flat-topped cells, or some round-topped cells, not appearing spongy
13,0$ Thallus narrow, forming mats of Y-shaped plants ; either floating or on periodically flooded mud $Riccia fluitans
13,14$ Thallus with sort, wide branches, plants ± forming rosettes ; on soil
14,0$ V-shaped groove in upper surface of thallus ; thallus edges curved up ; clear margin of long, narrow cells along sides of thallus (hand lens) $Riccia sorocarpa
14,0$ Wide, shallow groove in upper surface of thallus ; thallus edges curved down ; no clear margin along sides of thallus (hand lens) $Riccia glauca
15,16$ Thallus < 2 mm wide (check in case of branches of Pellia endiviifolia)
15,19$ Thallus 3 mm wide or more
16,17$ Midrib strongly differentiated from much thinner margins of thallus ; growing on trees or rocks
16,0$ Midrib lacking ; growing in marshes, on wet soil, etc. $Riccardia spp.
17,0$ Entire plant covered with short hairs, making a look furry $Apometzgeria pubescens
17,18$ Hairs restricted to margins and underside of plant
18,0$ Narrow shoot tip with abundant gemmae ; tip sticking away from substrate $Metzgeria fruticulosa/M. temperata
18,0$ Shoot tip usually without gemmae (occasionally a few) ; tip not narrower than rest of thallus, not sticking away from substrate $Metzgeria furcata
19,20$ Midrib well-defined (obvious when viewed from underside of thallus)
19,23$ Midrib not well-defined
20,0$ Thallus with narrow, forking branches at the tip $Pellia endiviifolia
20,21$ Thallus without forking branches at the tip
21,0$ Plant monoicous (female part in a flap on upper side of thallus ; male parts in small volcano-like hollows on same thallus ; non-fertile plants should be recorded as Pellia sp.) $Pellia epiphylla
21,22$ Plant dioicous (female part in a short tube on upper side of thallus ; male parts in small volcano-like hollows on separate thallus ; non-fertile plants should be recorded as Pellia sp.)
22,0$ Female plants with tubes that have fringed mouths $Pellia endiviifolia
22,0$ Female plants with tubes that have mouths which are slightly wavy $Pellia neesiana
23,0$ Plants vivid mid-green, with a greasy appearance ; thalli parallel-sided ; margins curved upwards ; fertile parts on thallus sides ; capsules egg-shaped, held on a colourless seta $Aneura pinguis
23,0$ Plant forming dark green rosettes ; margins not curved upwards ; fertile parts in pits on upper surface ; capsules long, horn-like and green $Anthoceros spp./Phaeoceros spp.
24,30$ Leaves divided into 2 unequal lobes, the smaller one lying more or less flat on or under the larger one, thus sometimes giving impression that leaves are in 4 ranks
24,25$ Leaves not divided into 2 unequal lobes, may be 2-toothed
25,26$ Leaves irregular, wavy, not distinctly separated from each other or from the stem ; appearance like a small lettuce
25,27$ Leaves in regular rows and quite distinct from stem
26,0$ Plant pale or dark green ; rhizoids purple ; on soil (only identifiable to species using spores – collect ripe, black capsules) $Fossombronia spp.
26,0$ Plant bright green ; rhizoids pale ; in dune slacks $Petalophyllum ralfsii
27,28$ Leaves split into many finely divided parts, sometimes appearing almost furry
27,29$ Leaves not finely divided ; may have toothed or entire margins
28,0$ Plant forming strikingly pale whitish-green patches, with bi- or tripinnately branched shoots 5-12 cm long $Trichocolea tomentella
28,0$ Plant reddish-brown (or yellow, orange, or olive-green), with once pinnate to bipinnate shoots ; leaf lobes fringed with long teeth $Ptilidium ciliare
29,45$ Leaves 2-, 3-, or 4-cleft, more or less symmetrical
29,65$ Leaves simple and rounded, the margins either entire or with numerous, minute teeth
30,31$ Smaller lobe lying on top of larger lobe, distinct when plant in situ
30,38$ Smaller lobe under larger lobe, not visible when plant in situ
31,0$ Each leaf divided to near base into 2 narrow oblong elements ; very common on acidic banks, rocks, etc. $Diplophyllum albicans
31,32$ Each leaf divided to about 1/3 or less, the segments round or nearly so, not narrowly oblong ; habitats various
32,0$ Smaller lobe only marginally smaller than larger one $Scapania compacta
32,33$ Smaller lobe < 3/4 of the size of the larger one
33,34$ Shoot tip bearing conspicuous clusters of red or brown gemmae
33,35$ Gemmae green (if present)
34,0$ Leaf lobes rounded or sometimes pointed, not tapering to a point ; gemmae brown $Scapania nemorea
34,0$ Leaf lobes tapering to a sharp point ; gemmae bright red $Scapania umbrosa
35,0$ Larger leaf lobe with base running down stem (hand lens, view from behind) ; smaller leaf lobe with base not running down stem $Scapania undulata
35,36$ Either both leaf lobes have bases running down the stem, or neither does
36,37$ Smaller leaf lobe crossing beyond the stem by a long way
36,0$ Smaller leaf lobe not crossing the stem $Scapania irrigua
37,0$ Tiny teeth visible at base of smaller lobe (x20 hand lens), just above junction with stem ; acid-lover, typical of wooded valleys $Scapania gracilis
37,0$ No teeth at base of smaller lobe ; lime-lover, typical of humid limestone or lime-rich sandstone $Scapania aspera
38,0$ Colonies composed of red/purple, cylindrical worm-like shoots $Pleurozia purpurea
38,39$ Shoots not cylindrical and worm-like ; usually tightly pressed to a substrate
39,0$ Shoots forming black patches, tipped yellowish-green, on limestone or lime-rich rock $Marchesinia mackaii
39,40$ Shoots not black
40,41$ Plants yellowish-green ; leaves with basal part folded over underneath, but without lobule
40,43$ Plants dark green, brownish, reddish, etc. ; leaves with a lobule on the lower surface, attached to the stem only at the base
41,0$ Plants minute (shoots < 0.5 mm wide) ; tiny, oval leaves spaced out along the stem like beads $Cololejeunea minutissimum/Microlejeunea
41,42$ Plants larger (shoots at least 1 mm wide)
42,0$ Shoots about 1 mm wide ; leaves with angle on back edge ; underleaves present $Lejeunea spp.
42,0$ Shoots about 2 mm wide ; leaves round, with no angle ; underleaves absent $Radula complanata
43,0$ Plant deep green when dry, vivid green when moist ; edges of many leaves curled upwards, giving shoots an untidy appearance from above $Porella platyphylla
43,44$ Plant usually reddish or brown, occasionally green ; leaf edges not curled upwards, so leaves look like neat scales
44,0$ Lobule as wide as long ; common on trees and branches, growing appressed to bark $Frullania dilatata
44,0$ Lobule much longer than wide ; frequent on rocks, where often grows appressed ; on trees and branches in western Britain, usually hanging loose from the bark $Frullania tamarisci
45,46$ Plants with a strong, peppery or must smell (some people cannot detect it ; if in doubt then look up these very common species) ; leaves 2-lobed
45,47$ Plants with no distinctive smell ; leaves 2-, 3- or 4-lobed
46,0$ Some leaves entire, others with 2 sharp lobes $Lophocolea heterophylla
46,0$ All leaves with 2 sharp lobes $Lophocolea bidentata
47,48$ Individual leaves scarcely visible with the naked eye ; like tiny hands, with 3 or 4 symmetrical lobes (x20 hand lens)
47,50$ Individual leaves easily visible with the naked eye, or if not so then with 2 symmetrical lobes
48,0$ Leaves with 3 or 4 'fingers' shorter than the 'palm' ; branching pinnate $Lepidozia reptans
48,49$ Leaves with 3 or 4 'fingers' clearly longer than the 'palm' ; branching irregular
49,0$ Leaves composed entirely of 'fingers' ; a species of dry but humid places $Blepharostoma trichophyllum
49,0$ Leaves with a short but distinct 'hand' ; species of bogs, peat or humus $Kurzia spp.
50,0$ Tip of each leaf split into 3 small but distinct teeth ; leaf tapering from very wide base, asymmetrical ; very robust, olive-green species of humid woods, etc. (shoots up to 5 mm across) $Bazzania trilobata
50,51$ Leaves more or less symmetrical : either showing 2 shallow teeth at tip only, or more deeply divided into 2 or 3 lobes
51,52$ Leaves almost flat, with the leading edge lying on top of the next leaf up the shoot ; plants pale white- or blue-green
51,53$ Leaves concave, usually obviously so, or if flat then the leading edge of each leaf tucked under the next leaf up the shoot ; plants less obviously pale
52,0$ Tips of leaf lobes divergent, with a wide angle between them $Calypogeia arguta
52,0$ Tips of leaf lobes ending close together, with an acute or rounded angle between them $Calypogeia fissa
53,0$ Shoots almost flat ; leaves with 2 lobes pointing towards each other and with a wide, crescent-shaped gap between them $Cephalozia connivens/C. lunulifolia
53,54$ Shoots with somewhat to strongly concave leaves ; lobes usually not pointing towards each other ; gap between lobes various
54,55$ Leaves divided more than halfway to the base into 2 lobes ; plants very slender, with shoots < 2 mm wide
54,57$ Leaves divided to much less than halfway to the base into 2 or 3 lobes
55,0$ Very long leaf lobes curve so the tips nearly meet ; shoots other bright red $Nowellia curvifolia
55,56$ Leaf lobes only slightly curved, tips more or less divergent ; shoots never red
56,0$ Stem translucent $Cephalozia bicuspidata
56,0$ Stem opaque $Cephaloziella spp.
57,58$ Many leaves with 3 lobes
57,60$ All leaves with 2 lobes
58,0$ Shoot tip clearly different from the remainder of the shoot, with tightly pressed, narrowed leaves, tipped with pale gemmae $Barbilophozia attenuata
58,59$ Shoot tip not differentiated ; gemmae absent or present
59,0$ One leaf edge much longer than the other, so that the 3 lobes point 90° to the stem ; gemmae occasionally present $Tritomaria quinquedentata
59,0$ Both leaf edges of similar length, so the 3 lobes point about 45° to the stem ; gemmae absent $Barbilophozia floerkei
60,61$ Gemmae conspicuous on leaf tip
60,63$ Gemmae absent from leaf tip
61,62$ Gemmae green or very pale
61,0$ Gemmae red or brown $Lophozia excisa/L. bicrenata/L. sudetica
62,0$ Underside of stem brown ; plants bright mid-green ; leaves with 2 lobes, but no extra teeth $Lophozia ventricosa
62,0$ Entire stem green ; plants very pale green ; leaves with 2 lobes, and with extra teeth on leaf margins (hand lens) $Lophozia incisa
63,64$ Leaves with broadly rounded lobes ; leaf edges not recurved
63,0$ Leaves with sharply pointed lobes ; x20 hand lens shows that at least some leaves have recurved edges $Marsupella emarginata
64,0$ Plant dark green, brown or black ; inflated perianths < 2 times as long as wide ; mostly in acidic habitats $Gymnocolea inflata
64,0$ Plant pale green, often looking very translucent ; perianths tubular, > 2 times as long as wide ; mostly in lime-rich habitats $Leiocolea turbinata
65,0$ Robust (shoot 3 mm wide), variegated red and yellow-green ; forming conspicuous, erect cushions $Mylia taylorii
65,66$ Not red and not forming cushions
66,67$ Shoot usually with obvious gemmae
66,69$ Gemmae absent
67,0$ Leaves with the leading edge lying on top of the next leaf up the shoot $Calypogeia spp.
67,68$ Leaves with the leading edge of each leaf tucked under the next leaf up the shoot
68,0$ Gemmae produced on leaves that are clearly longer and more pointed than those on the rest of the shoot $Mylia anomala
68,0$ Gemmae produced on leaves that look the same as or smaller than those on the rest of the shoot $Odontoschisma denudatum
69,70$ Shoot laterally compressed, especially near tip, so that there are 2 ranks of leaves on opposite sides of the stem and more or less appressed to it
69,74$ Shoot dorsi-ventrally compressed, the 2 ranks of obliquely inserted leaves lying more or less flat, one on each side of the stem
70,0$ In Sphagnum bogs ; shoots usually orange-brown, with numerous very slender, thread-like branches that appear leafless and grow downwards from the stem $Odontoschisma sphagni
70,71$ Not in Sphagnum bogs ; very slender, thread-like, apparently leafless branches absent, any slender shoots not growing downwards
71,0$ Plants usually with some very slender, creeping shoots with small leaves ; leaf margins often with a border of enlarged cells (hand lens) $Jungermannia gracillima
71,72$ Very slender shoots absent ; border absent
72,0$ Leaves very slightly appressed to each other, so shoots appear strongly flattened and the stem is scarcely flattened and the stem is scarcely visible from above $Nardia compressa
72,73$ Leaves less tightly appressed ; stem clearly visible
73,0$ Underleaves present (x20 hand lens ; check carefully the shoot tip) $Nardia scalaris
73,0$ Underleaves absent $Jungermannia spp.
74,75$ Leaves convex, with one margin extending down onto stem
74,77$ Leaves flat, margin not turning down along stem
75,0$ Leaves entire ; plants of very wet places $Chiloscyphus spp.
75,76$ Leaf margin toothed, although teeth may be very sparse ; plants of humid but dry places
76,0$ Leaves broadly rounded ; leaves not deciduous ; plants unscented $Plagiochila asplenioides/P. porelloides
76,0$ Leaves tapering to a squared-off or 2-lobed tip ; leaves often deciduous ; plants scented when lightly crushed ; restricted to western Britain $Plagiochila killarniensis/P. punctata/P. spinulosa
77,0$ Leaves in opposite pairs on stem ; relatively long and narrow ; underleaves joined to lateral leaves (hand lens), and toothed along margin but not notched at tip $Saccogyna viticulosa
77,0$ Leaves alternate along stem ; relatively short and broad ; underleaves free, broadly rounded, with (sometimes shallow) notch at tip $Calypogeia spp.
78,88$ Leaves with very obvious vertical plates of green tissue on upper surface of the nerve (hand lens), either closely packed, making the leaf appear opaque, or loosely packed. – Polytrichum, Atrichum, etc.
78,79$ Leaves lacking vertical plates of green tissue on upper surface
79,80$ Leaves arranged strictly in 2 ranks
79,81$ Leaves not strictly in 2 ranks
80,96$ Shoot look like miniature ferns. – Fissidens, etc.
80,0$ Shoots with very long, slender leaves, not looking like miniature ferns $Distichium spp.
81,104$ Some or all of the leaves ending in almost white hair point. – Grimmia, Tortula, etc.
81,82$ Leaves lacking almost white hair point
82,0$ Dense cushions whitish-green when dry ; leaves broadly spearhead-shaped, blunt at tip, 6-8 mm long, appearing nerveless $Leucobryum spp.
82,83$ Colonies not whitish-green when dry ; leaves not appearing nerveless
83,0$ Leaf tip almost white, making whole shoots appear silvery ; shoots with appressed leaves, making them catkin-like $Bryum argenteum
83,84$ Whole leaf green, brownish, almost black, etc., but not looking silvery
84,85$ Capsules disproportionately large compared with leafy parts
84,86$ Capsules not disproportionately large, or absent
85,0$ Capsule on a long seta ; leaves almost invisible $Buxbaumia spp.
85,0$ Capsules on a very short seta, looking like cereal grains surrounded by strap-like leaves $Diphyscium foliosum
86,126$ Leaves narrowly spearhead-shaped, > 5 times as long as wide ; each leaf tapering to a long, fine point into which the nerve runs. – Dicranella, Dicranum, etc.
86,87$ Leaves either with rounded outlines, or parallel-sided, or if gradually tapering then not > 5 times as long as wide
87,156$ Leaves large to very large (largest leaves 3.5-10 mm long and 1-3 mm wide) ; plants mostly robust (sometimes bud-like or forming short rosettes), in wide patches rather than compact cushions. – Mnium, Plagiomnium, etc.
87,0$ Leaves medium-sized to very small (3-5 mm long or less, most commonly 1-2 mm long and seldom as much as 1 mm wide) ; plants mostly small and slender (occasionally tall and small-leaved) ; habit various, often forming compact cushions, sometimes forming turfs or scattered shoots. This large section is subdivided into five, according to habitat, growing on : 
87,172$ * tarmac or concrete paths
87,181$ * walls in towns and villages, on bridge parapets, etc.
87,196$ * rocks, including surfaces of boulders, cliffs and drystone walls
87,225$ * soil, including gravely detritus, sand, clay, loam, peaty humus, soil-capped walls, and thin soil on roadsides or paths
87,267$ * trees, logs, fence posts or decaying tree stumps
88,89$ Margins of leaves inrolled and untoothed (hand lens) ; shoots 2 mm-7 cm tall
88,92$ Margins of leaves plane and toothed (hand lens) ; shoots 1-25 cm tall
89,0$ Scattered, tiny plants (2-4 mm across) on lime-rich soil $Aloina spp.
89,90$ Scattered plants (> 5 mm across) or dense patches on acidic soil
90,0$ Longest leaves < 3 mm ; vertical plates of green tissue on upper surface of the nerve few, wavy and loosely packed ; north-western $Oligotrichum hercynicum
90,91$ Leaves longer ; vertical plates of green tissue on upper surface of the nerve dense, straight and crowded
91,0$ Leaves with almost white hair point $Polytrichum piliferum
91,0$ Almost white hair point lacking ; leaves ending in short but acute, brown point $Polytrichum juniperinum
92,0$ Each leaf with fewer than 5 loosely packed, vertical plates of green tissue restricted to the upper surface of the nerve $Atrichum undulatum
92,93$ 25+ densely packed plates covering almost entire leaf surface
93,0$ Plant pale bluish-green (glaucous) above $Pogonatum urnigerum
93,94$ Plant mid-green or dark green above
94,0$ Shoots 0.5-1 cm tall, leaves forming a rosette $Pogonatum aloides
94,95$ Shoots 3-25 cm tall, leaves along stem
95,0$ Chiefly on wet moorland ; leaf sheath glossy (visible when upper leaves are stripped off a stem) ; plant often > 15 cm tall $Polytrichum commune
95,0$ Chiefly in woods ; leaf sheath dull ; plants rarely > 9 cm tall $Polytrichastrum formosum
96,0$ Leaves nerveless ; protonema shining green like glow-worm tails ; scarce plant of caves, rabbit holes, etc. $Schistostega pennata
96,97$ Leaves nerved to tip or near it ; protonema not shining	97
97,0$ Minute (shoots < 5 mm long) ; fruiting plants with < 5 pairs of leaves (non-fruiting plants unidentifiable) $Fissidens exilis/F. pusillus/F. viridulus
97,98$ Larger (shoots 5 mm or longer) ; fruiting plants with > 5 pairs of leaves
98,0$ On rocks and masonry in rivers or streams $Fissidens crassipes/F. rufulus/F. rivularis
98,99$ Not on rocks in rivers or streams
99,100$ Leaves bordered with long, narrow cells (hand lens) ; small (stems 0.5-2 cm) ; seta terminal
99,101$ Leaves not bordered (hand lens) ; larger (stems 1-8 cm) ; seta various
100,0$ Capsule upright (not conclusively identifiable without capsules) $Fissidens bryoides
100,0$ Capsule inclined, more or less horizontal (not conclusively identifiable without capsules) $Fissidens incurvus/F. curnovii
101,0$ Nerve ending at leaf tip in a short, protruding point ; seta basal $Fissidens taxifolius
101,102$ Nerve ending below leaf tip ; seta terminal or lateral
102,0$ Shoots about 4 mm wide ; leaves down-curved at tip, giving shoots rounded backs ; leaf margins untoothed ; seta terminal $Fissidens osmundoides
102,103$ Shoots flat, about 8 mm wide ; leaf margins irregularly toothed ; seta lateral
103,0$ Leaves with very distinct pale marginal band ; on dry, calcareous rocks (needs checking with microscope) $Fissidens dubius
103,0$ Leaves with indistinct or no pale marginal band ; in flushes, fens or on wet, calcareous rocks (needs checking with microscope) $Fissidens adianthoides
104,0$ Nerve projecting from leaf tip as a fine, greenish hair ; leaves corkscrew-curled when dry $Bryum capillare
104,105$ Nerve projecting as a colourless or white, very distinct hair point ; leaves not corkscrew-curled when dry
105,106$ Leaves very long, narrow and sword-shaped
105,108$ Leaves relatively shorter – roughly triangular or tongue-shaped
106,0$ Plants dark, blackish-green ; leaves 6-9 mm long, with long, untidy-looking hair point $Campylopus atrovirens
106,107$ Plants light or mid-green ; leaves 4-5 mm long, with relatively short hair point
107,0$ Hair points bent back when dry, looking star-like ; nerve > 1/4 of leaf width at base (hand lens) $Campylopus introflexus
107,0$ Hair points not bent back when dry ; nerve < 1/4 of leaf width at base (hand lens) $Campylopus brevipilus
108,109$ On trees or fence posts
108,113$ On some other substrate
109,0$ Prominent line of powder-like gemmae on upper side of nerve $Syntrichia papillosa
109,110$ Gemmae absent from leaves
110,0$ Capsules on curved-seta (straight when old and dry) $Grimmia pulvinata
110,111$ Capsules with straight, sometimes very short, seta
111,0$ Leaves tapering into wide-based hair point ; capsule scarcely raised above leaves $Orthotrichum diaphanum
111,112$ Narrow hair point projects from middle of broad leaf tip ; capsule (if present) on seta long enough to hold capsule base clear of leaves
112,0$ Hair point smooth (hand lens) $Syntrichia laevipila
112,0$ Hair point with sharp teeth throughout its length (hand lens) $Syntrichia virescens
113,0$ On sand dunes ; plans bright golden green to orange-brown when moist ; leaves strongly bent back, tapering to a narrow tip, with strongly recurved margins $Syntrichia ruralis subsp. ruraliformis
113,114$ Some other habitat and appearance
114,115$ Forming straggling patches ; stems freely branched
114,117$ Forming neat tufts or cushions ; stems little branched (mostly simple or merely forked)
115,0$ Hair point with narrow teeth at 90° to base (hand lens) ; plants very robust ; forming loose carpets, 20 cm to 1 m or more across, on peat or rock (individual stems often 12-25 cm long) $Racomitrium lanuginosum
115,116$ Hair point with forward-pointing teeth ; plants much more slender, in loose tufts or thin, straggling patches a few centimeters across, on rock (individual stems 2-8 cm)
116,0$ Leaves very concave, with rounded backs and no nerve ; capsules on short seta, hidden among leaves $Hedwigia stellata
116,0$ Leaves flat or with keeled backs and an obvious nerve ; capsules on long seta (> 5 times length of capsule) $Racomitrium heterostichum agg.
117,118$ Capsules always present (search carefully for old ones) on short seta so capsule base is hidden among sheathing leaves
117,119$ Capsules (if present) on seta long enough to hold capsule base clear of leaves
118,0$ Plants squat and unbranched ; old capsules with folds when dry ; young capsules with slightly hairy calyptra $Orthotrichum diaphanum
118,0$ Plants loosely branches ; old capsules smooth ; calyptra without hairs $Schistidium crassipilum
119,120$ Leaves abruptly narrowing into hair point (may be parallel-sided or tapering before abrupt narrowing)
119,123$ Leaves gradually tapering into hair point
120,121$ Hair point smooth (hand lens)
120,122$ Hair point with sharp teeth throughout its length (hand lens)
121,0$ Leaves tongue-shaped, appearing thickened on margins (hand lens) ; capsules on long (> 1.5 cm) straight seta $Tortula muralis
121,0$ Leaves gradually tapering from base, then abruptly narrowing into hair point ; margins recurved, but not thickened ; capsules on short (< 8 mm) curved seta (NB : straightens when dry) $Grimmia pulvinata
122,0$ Leaf margins recurved for most of its length ; upper part of leaves curved upwards and inwards $Syntrichia ruralis subsp. ruralis
122,0$ Leaf margins not recurved ; upper part of leaves flat $Syntrichia intermedia
123,0$ Shoots with abundant, short branches ; usually on gravelly soil, mine waste, etc. $Racomitrium ericoides
123,124$ Shoots with long, irregular branches or unbranched ; on rock
124,0$ Tiny (< 8 mm deep), almost black cushions ; almost always with pale green capsules on short (as long as capsule), straight seta $Grimmia donniana
124,125$ Cushions at least 1 cm deep ; seta either curved or much longer than capsules
125,0$ Capsules on long, straight seta ; hair point flattened where it joins the rest of the leaf $Racomitrium heterostichum
125,0$ Capsules on short, curved seta (but rarely produced) ; hair point not flattened where it joins the rest of the leaf $Grimmia trichophylla
126,127$ Robust, 2.5-15 cm tall
126,137$ Small, up to 2 cm tall
127,128$ Leaves strongly curved and turned to one side, at least at the shoot tip
127,130$ Leaves straight
128,0$ Leaves very long (the longest being 10-14 mm), all sickle-shaped and very strongly turned to one side of shoot ; chiefly in sheltered, humid woods $Dicranum majus
128,129$ Leaves shorter (the longest 6-8 mm), less strongly and regularly turned to one side of shoot ; habitat various	129
